## 5. Functional Requirements

### 5.1. Project Intake & Plan Processing Module
- **FR-01: Multi-Format Ingestion:** System must accept PDF, DWG, DXF, RVT (Revit), IFC, JPG, PNG, and TIFF files. Users can upload files individually or in bulk (.zip).
- **FR-02: Document Classification AI:** Upon upload, a fine-tuned computer vision model classifies sheets by type: Architectural Plan, Structural Plan, Civil Plan, MEP Plan, Title Sheet, Detail, Schedule, or Unrecognized. Confidence score displayed.
- **FR-03: Optical Character Recognition (OCR):** Extract all text from vector and raster PDFs, including handwritten notes (with confidence flag). Extracted text is stored as searchable metadata linked to the sheet.
- **FR-04: Named Entity Recognition (NER):** An NLP model extracts key construction scope entities from notes and schedules: "Relocate sprinkler main," "New 400A service," "Remove load-bearing wall," "New accessible ramp." These become "Scope Tags."
- **FR-05: Symbol Detection:** Model identifies standard architectural symbols indicating: Fire alarm devices, plumbing fixtures, structural steel moment frames, and egress doors. Symbols are flagged as potential permit triggers.
- **FR-06: Area & Dimension Extraction:** Auto-calculate total building area, floor areas, and identify if any dimension exceeds code thresholds (e.g., egress travel distance).
- **FR-07: Geospatial Analysis:** Parse project address and automatically overlay parcel data with FEMA Flood Maps, US Fish & Wildlife Wetlands Inventory, National Register of Historic Places districts, and state-specific coastal zone boundaries.
- **FR-08: Adaptive Project Wizard:** A smart questionnaire that adapts based on extracted data. Key questions: Project Valuation ($), Building Occupancy Classification (IBC Ch. 3), Construction Type (I-V), Work Type (New, Alteration L1/L2/L3, Addition, Change of Use), and whether work includes fire suppression, alarm, or structural modifications.

### 5.2. Regulatory Intelligence & Reasoning Engine
- **FR-09: Jurisdictional Rule Graph Database:** A Neo4j graph database storing permit triggers as nodes and conditions as edges. Example: [Node: Commercial Building Permit] –[TRIGGERED_IF]–> [Condition: Alteration Cost > $50,000 OR Structural Scope Tag Detected]. Each rule is versioned and source-linked.
- **FR-10: Deterministic Rules Engine:** The final permit requirement list is generated by executing the project's extracted scope against the deterministic rule graph. Generative AI is strictly prohibited from generating the final permit recommendation. It is used solely for unstructured data interpretation (plan notes).
- **FR-11: Citation Transparency:** Every permit recommendation must include: Permit Name, Issuing Authority, Triggering Scope Tag, Direct URL link to the adopted municipal code section, and last verified date.
- **FR-12: Fee Estimator:** Based on jurisdiction fee schedules and project valuation, provide a calculated permit fee estimate with a "Low-High" range.
- **FR-13: Timeline Estimator:** Based on jurisdiction-sourced data and historical user data (anonymized), estimate the review timeline (e.g., "Typical Building Permit: 3-4 weeks for in-depth review").

### 5.3. Compliance Locker & Portfolio Management
- **FR-14: Project Profile Save & Versioning:** All intake data, uploaded plans, extracted entities, and generated permit lists are saved as a versioned project record. Users can note "Bid Stage (v1)" vs "Permit Set (v2)" and re-run analysis.
- **FR-15: Scope Template Library:** Users can save any project's wizard answers and scope tags as a named template (e.g., "Standard Interior TI – Office"). This template is available in the wizard for any new project.
- **FR-16: Active Project Dashboard:** A Kanban-style board sorting projects by stage: Scoping, Permitting, Under Review, Approved, Construction, Closed.
- **FR-17: Compliance Health Score:** A 0-100 score for each project. Calculation: (Number of "Obtained" permits + "Not Applicable") / (Total Required Permits). A permit is "Required" until a user marks it N/A with a required text justification.

### 5.4. Code Change Radar™ (Proactive Monitoring)
- **FR-18: Code Watch Subscriptions:** The system automatically subscribes a project to watch the code sections cited in its required permits. Users can manually add additional sections.
- **FR-19: Regulatory Change Detection Engine:** A background process continuously monitors municipal websites, state code adoption portals, and legal databases for published amendments.
- **FR-20: Impact Correlation Alert:** When a change is detected in a watched code section, the system correlates it against the specific Scope Tags of all active projects. An alert is issued: "Change Detected: City Council adopted IBC 2024. Your project at 123 Main St. has a structural trigger (Shear Wall) that requires re-evaluation. See Section 1705.3."

### 5.5. Subcontractor Network & Trade Permits
- **FR-21: Role-Based Access Control (RBAC):** Project Owner (Full Access), Collaborator (Internal PM), Subcontractor (Trade-Specific View Only), Read-Only (Client/Owner).
- **FR-22: Trade Permit Assignment:** The Project Owner designates a Subcontractor user as "Responsible Party" for specific trade permits (E-01 Electrical, P-01 Plumbing).
- **FR-23: Digital Acknowledgement:** The designated Subcontractor receives an in-app notification and email. They must view their assigned permit and click "Acknowledged & In Progress." This action is logged in the immutable audit trail with a timestamp and user ID.
- **FR-24: Subcontractor Bulk View:** Subcontractors logging in see a simplified dashboard of *only* their assigned permits across all projects they've been invited to.

